1
00:00:34,320 --> 00:00:38,720
Every week we perform hundreds of different tasks in our laboratories.

2
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:44,160
This work is performed at different workstations, using different instruments and materials,

3
00:00:44,160 --> 00:00:47,720
and employing different movements.

4
00:00:47,720 --> 00:00:52,160
As a result, each task makes a different set of demands on our bodies.

5
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:58,800
If we aren't careful, some of them can lead to unhealthy stress or strain.

6
00:00:58,800 --> 00:01:03,260
The science of ergonomics helps us to take a look at these situations and determine

7
00:01:03,260 --> 00:01:07,020
what we need to do to avoid any harmful effects.

8
00:01:07,020 --> 00:01:15,780
It does this by examining our job, work area, instruments, and tools.

9
00:01:15,780 --> 00:01:21,180
Ergonomics shows us how to adapt what we do to our own physical makeup so we can work effectively,

10
00:01:21,180 --> 00:01:35,360
and most importantly, safely.

11
00:01:35,360 --> 00:01:37,320
Are you short or tall?

12
00:01:37,320 --> 00:01:39,240
Do you have long legs?

13
00:01:39,240 --> 00:01:40,820
What about your arms?

14
00:01:40,820 --> 00:01:46,820
Physical size and shape are important considerations when designing work areas and equipment.

15
00:01:46,820 --> 00:01:53,100
To work safely, we need to minimize the amount of stress we place on our bodies.

16
00:01:53,100 --> 00:01:58,060
The design engineers who develop workstations and other lab equipment try to make them work

17
00:01:58,060 --> 00:02:00,180
well for everyone.

18
00:02:00,180 --> 00:02:04,800
Since this is pretty much impossible, they do their best to come close by designing them

19
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:08,020
to fit most people.

20
00:02:08,020 --> 00:02:12,940
Customizing your work area so that it works best for you becomes a job for you and your

21
00:02:12,940 --> 00:02:14,740
supervisor.

22
00:02:14,740 --> 00:02:19,420
How closely you match up with your workplace will determine how hard your muscles and joints

23
00:02:19,420 --> 00:02:23,100
will be asked to work while you're on the job.

24
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:27,540
To start, think about arranging your instruments and materials so you're not forced to make

25
00:02:27,540 --> 00:02:30,620
unhealthy movements such as overstretching.

26
00:02:30,620 --> 00:02:37,140
You also want to avoid long sessions of repetitive motion without rest, irregular and extreme

27
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:43,200
motions, overly heavy loads, and using the wrong tool for the job.

28
00:02:43,200 --> 00:02:49,670
These are the main contributors to ergonomic health problems.

29
00:02:49,670 --> 00:02:54,070
Practicing positive work techniques whenever possible is one thing that can help us with

30
00:02:54,070 --> 00:02:56,270
these problems.

31
00:02:56,270 --> 00:03:01,230
This includes performing stretching exercises to relieve muscle tension and doing our best

32
00:03:01,230 --> 00:03:16,960
to keep our bodies in neutral, non-stressful positions.

33
00:03:16,960 --> 00:03:20,420
Neutral positions put the least stress possible on our body.

34
00:03:20,420 --> 00:03:25,300
Each major joint and muscle group has its own neutral position.

35
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:27,120
We'll start with the wrist.

36
00:03:27,120 --> 00:03:31,600
Keep your wrist straight, as if you were about to shake hands with someone.

37
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:36,680
This position creates the least amount of stress, and you should use it whenever possible.

38
00:03:36,680 --> 00:03:40,860
You'll find that other wrist positions are significantly more stressful, especially when

39
00:03:40,860 --> 00:03:45,000
they're used over long periods of time.

40
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,380
There's also a neutral position to help your arms and shoulders.

41
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:52,440
If you're working at a lab bench, try to keep your upper arms to the side and your

42
00:03:52,440 --> 00:03:55,020
forearms at 90 degree angles.

43
00:03:55,020 --> 00:04:00,170
This puts the least amount of stress on these joints and muscles.

44
00:04:00,170 --> 00:04:05,130
It may not seem like there are many opportunities to use neutral positions, but in reality,

45
00:04:05,130 --> 00:04:06,730
there are a lot.

46
00:04:06,730 --> 00:04:10,490
Paying attention to workstation height is one of the keys.

47
00:04:10,490 --> 00:04:16,430
You need to fine-tune your workstation to fit your physical characteristics.

48
00:04:16,430 --> 00:04:22,530
Keep your work surfaces at a height that makes it easy to maintain neutral arm positioning.

49
00:04:22,530 --> 00:04:27,730
You may need to raise or lower your chair to get the best position possible.

50
00:04:27,730 --> 00:04:32,250
If you're standing and need to get higher, use a small step stool or platform, or see

51
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:46,760
if you can lower the work surface.

52
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:51,720
As we've said, careful positioning of your tools and materials is important to good ergonomic

53
00:04:51,720 --> 00:04:53,340
health as well.

54
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:55,800
Keep them in front of your body as much as possible.

55
00:04:55,800 --> 00:04:59,640
This will encourage neutral movements.

56
00:04:59,640 --> 00:05:03,760
Never put supplies where you have to stretch to reach them, especially if you need to support

57
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:08,120
yourself with one hand to grab your materials with the other.

58
00:05:08,120 --> 00:05:12,480
Avoid other stretching and twisting movements, and don't put yourself in cramped positions

59
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:16,880
that will prevent your body from moving efficiently.

60
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:21,840
Another ergonomic problem involves repetition, such as extending your arms in the same way

61
00:05:21,840 --> 00:05:25,760
over and over again for long periods of time.

62
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:30,480
Repetition can lead to fatigue, which is a signal that your body needs some relief.

63
00:05:30,480 --> 00:05:35,520
To guard against this, vary your work patterns when you can, and take periodic mini-breaks

64
00:05:35,520 --> 00:05:39,520
to loosen tight muscles.

65
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:42,200
Never use excessive force to perform a task.

66
00:05:42,200 --> 00:05:46,560
Applying a lot of pressure to any part of your body can lead to an injury, especially

67
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:50,600
if you're not in a neutral position.

68
00:05:50,600 --> 00:05:53,760
Much of the lab work we perform is done with gloves.

69
00:05:53,760 --> 00:05:58,960
Choosing a pair with the right fit is essential to ergonomic safety.

70
00:05:58,960 --> 00:06:03,440
Gloves that are too large or too thick force you to grip objects tightly, which can lead

71
00:06:03,440 --> 00:06:07,240
to painful swelling of your tendons.

72
00:06:07,240 --> 00:06:12,060
Gloves that are too tight or are stiff at the wrist can press on nerves, blood vessels,

73
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:16,820
and the carpal tunnel, again leading to serious injuries.

74
00:06:16,820 --> 00:06:21,420
So make sure you select gloves that are comfortable and that fit you well for the work you're

75
00:06:21,420 --> 00:06:34,360
performing.

76
00:06:34,360 --> 00:06:37,520
There are also ergonomic issues involving your eyes.

77
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:42,460
For instance, working for long hours at a microscope or computer can lead to an uncomfortable

78
00:06:42,460 --> 00:06:44,800
case of eye strain.

79
00:06:44,800 --> 00:06:47,800
To avoid this, take a comfort break.

80
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:50,200
Close your eyes and rest them for a minute.

81
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:53,360
One technique is to cover them with the palms of your hands.

82
00:06:53,360 --> 00:06:58,200
Do this periodically to keep your eyes feeling fresh.

83
00:06:58,200 --> 00:07:02,520
An exercise called refocusing can often help as well.

84
00:07:02,520 --> 00:07:06,440
Move your eyes away from your equipment to something more distant, like a point outside

85
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,820
your window or across the lab.

86
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:13,440
Stay focused on this point for 60 to 90 seconds.

87
00:07:13,440 --> 00:07:18,920
Both of these practices allow your eye muscles to relax, back to a more normal state before

88
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:33,520
you return to the close-in focus of your work.

89
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:37,600
Your back and neck are especially vulnerable to ergonomic problems.

90
00:07:37,600 --> 00:07:40,880
You need to consider them both when working in the lab.

91
00:07:40,880 --> 00:07:45,000
Stress to either can lead to painful injuries.

92
00:07:45,000 --> 00:07:53,600
Your back can be weakened in many ways through improper lifting, a fall, even bad posture.

93
00:07:53,600 --> 00:07:57,600
As with the rest of your body, you also need to keep your back and neck in a neutral

94
00:07:57,600 --> 00:07:59,160
straight position.

95
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:03,280
That means no unusual bending or twisting.

96
00:08:03,280 --> 00:08:08,160
Believe it or not, sitting is one of the most stressful positions for your back.

97
00:08:08,160 --> 00:08:12,480
Remember to sit so that your lower back, its lumbar region, is comfortably supported by

98
00:08:12,480 --> 00:08:14,580
the padding of your chair.

99
00:08:14,580 --> 00:08:18,680
Keep both feet flat on the floor, knees slightly higher than your hips.

100
00:08:18,680 --> 00:08:22,720
This position places the least stress on your back.

101
00:08:22,720 --> 00:08:27,640
To get the best lumbar support, you may need to adjust your chair or place a pillow

102
00:08:27,640 --> 00:08:30,600
or rolled-up towel behind your back.

103
00:08:30,600 --> 00:08:35,560
If your feet tend to dangle, you should rest them on a book or platform.

104
00:08:35,560 --> 00:08:40,240
While they're better than they used to be, many lab stools still lack good lumbar support.

105
00:08:40,240 --> 00:08:44,120
Here, shifting your hips forward will help to keep you from hunching over.

106
00:08:44,120 --> 00:08:48,000
Again, a towel or pillow may be helpful too.

107
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,280
Use the stool's rails as foot rests and take frequent stretches to loosen up tight

108
00:08:52,280 --> 00:08:55,240
muscles.

109
00:08:55,240 --> 00:08:59,560
Standing can be just as tiring to your back as it is to your feet and legs.

110
00:08:59,640 --> 00:09:04,040
Again, organize your tools and materials ahead of time so that everything's well

111
00:09:04,040 --> 00:09:05,160
within reach.

112
00:09:05,160 --> 00:09:07,960
Avoid bending and stooping.

113
00:09:07,960 --> 00:09:11,720
Keep comfort in mind when you're standing for long periods of time.

114
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:14,240
Shift your weight from one foot to the other.

115
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:17,720
Using a foot rest to keep one foot higher than the other can also help to

116
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:20,360
take some of the load off your back.

117
00:09:20,360 --> 00:09:25,560
Wear comfortable shoes with cushioned insoles and stand on cushioned anti-fatigue mats

118
00:09:25,560 --> 00:09:27,640
if they're available.

119
00:09:27,640 --> 00:09:43,440
You'll be amazed at how much difference these types of things can make.

120
00:09:43,440 --> 00:09:49,080
Proper lifting techniques are essential to the health of your back and neck.

121
00:09:49,080 --> 00:09:53,080
Before lifting an object, examine it to make sure you're not biting off more

122
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:54,960
than you can chew.

123
00:09:54,960 --> 00:09:59,200
If you can safely lift the object yourself, first get close to it.

124
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:02,360
Keeping your back straight, bend slowly at the knees.

125
00:10:02,360 --> 00:10:04,520
Don't bend at the waist.

126
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:06,880
Get a good grip on the sides of the object.

127
00:10:06,880 --> 00:10:10,880
Lift slowly with your legs, keeping your back straight and balancing the load on

128
00:10:10,880 --> 00:10:12,880
your chest.

129
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,760
When walking with an object, remember not to twist your back.

130
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:21,560
Turn with your feet instead and always keep the object close to you.

131
00:10:21,560 --> 00:10:25,560
This also helps to reduce the strain on your back.

132
00:10:25,560 --> 00:10:29,800
To set the object down, simply follow the rules in reverse.

133
00:10:29,800 --> 00:10:36,570
Keep your back straight, bend with your knees and set the object down carefully.

134
00:10:36,570 --> 00:10:40,410
If something's too heavy or awkward to handle, don't risk an injury.

135
00:10:40,410 --> 00:10:41,690
Get help.

136
00:10:41,690 --> 00:10:43,730
Lift the object together.

137
00:10:43,730 --> 00:10:47,890
Counting out loud is often helpful.

138
00:10:47,890 --> 00:10:52,170
If there's no one around to help or the load is too heavy for two people to carry,

139
00:10:52,170 --> 00:10:55,770
get a cart or dolly.

140
00:10:55,770 --> 00:11:00,050
Exercise can be a big help in avoiding ergonomic injuries.

141
00:11:00,050 --> 00:11:03,970
A regular exercise regimen will keep your body in good condition

142
00:11:03,970 --> 00:11:07,850
and enable your muscles to do what you ask of them.

143
00:11:07,850 --> 00:11:11,610
It's also a good idea to start your day off with some warm-up stretches.

144
00:11:11,610 --> 00:11:15,410
And remember to do a few limbering exercises during your breaks.

145
00:11:15,410 --> 00:11:18,970
They should easily fit into your work schedule and help keep you comfortable

146
00:11:18,970 --> 00:11:25,020
throughout the day.

147
00:11:25,020 --> 00:11:28,900
You can help to eliminate ergonomic aches and pains by paying attention

148
00:11:28,900 --> 00:11:32,140
to your body mechanics and your work environment.

149
00:11:32,140 --> 00:11:34,940
Let's review.

150
00:11:34,940 --> 00:11:39,910
Arrange your materials so that they're within easy reach.

151
00:11:39,910 --> 00:11:43,550
Raise or lower work surfaces and position chairs and stools

152
00:11:43,550 --> 00:11:47,880
so that you can use neutral positions.

153
00:11:47,880 --> 00:11:53,600
Avoid repetitive motions and take short breaks throughout the day.

154
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:58,410
Make sure that you have good lumbar support when you're sitting.

155
00:11:58,410 --> 00:12:00,410
Practice good lifting habits.

156
00:12:00,410 --> 00:12:04,690
Lift with your legs, not with your back.

157
00:12:04,690 --> 00:12:08,820
And get plenty of exercise.

158
00:12:08,820 --> 00:12:10,900
It's easy to work ergonomically.

159
00:12:10,900 --> 00:12:15,140
Just follow the rules and use common sense.

160
00:12:15,140 --> 00:12:19,620
And you'll go home feeling great every day.
